WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:10.240
<v Ira Glass>The question started right after Tobin and his husband moved to
<v Ira Glass>这个问题出现在托宾和他丈夫搬到湾区并一起买了房子之后。

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:16.410
<v Ira Glass>the Bay Area and got a house together.
<v Ira Glass>湾区，然后一起买了房子。

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:24.950
<v Ira Glass>Tobin's family was pretty excited about this. They all live within an hour. And they brought meals over for weeks. His mom bought them shades. But this question popped up.
<v Ira Glass>托宾的家人对此感到非常兴奋。他们都住在离那不到一小时路程的地方。他们连续几周给他们送饭。他妈妈给他们买了窗帘。但这个问题突然冒了出来。

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:34.190
<v Tobin  Low>And the first time I noticed it happening, it was with my aunt. Kind of out of nowhere, she was like, oh, Which one of you is handy? Is one of you handy?
<v Tobin  Low>我第一次注意到这种情况是在我姑妈身上。她毫无预兆地问：“噢，你们俩谁比较擅长做家务？或者谁比较手巧？”

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:38.120
<v Tobin  Low>And I was just like, why does she want to know that? Why does she care?
<v Tobin  Low>我当时就在想，她为什么想知道这个？她为什么要在意这个？

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:39.740
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是啊，是啊。

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:56.580
<v Tobin Low>And I had, like, feelings about it, and I couldn't tell why. And then, it just kind of kept happening with other family members. Like, they would be talking about, oh, you guys moved in together into this house. Which one of you is handy?
<v Tobin Low>我对这个问题有些想法，却说不出为什么。后来，这种情况在其他家庭成员身上也接连发生。比如，他们会聊到：“噢，你们一起搬进这所房子了。你们俩谁比较手巧？”

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:07.750
<v Tobin Low>And on its face, it was kind of like, oh, we know when you're in a house, there's a lot of things to fix and a lot of things to do. But it felt like there was something else happening there, and it kind of bothered me.
<v Tobin Low>表面上看，这好像是说：“噢，我们知道你们住进新房，有很多东西要修理，有很多事要做。”但总觉得背后还有别的意思，这让我有点困扰。

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:12.570
<v Ira Glass>Something else there, like there was a question underneath the question that they were trying to get the answer to.
<v Ira Glass>背后有别的意思，就好像他们问的问题下面还藏着另一个他们想得到答案的问题。

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:33.220
<v Tobin Low>Yeah, like there was something else trying to be figured out. And I don't know. Like, the more I thought about it and why I was having feelings about it, it was kind of like this weird aha moment of, like, oh, I think you're asking who the man is in my relationship.
<v Tobin Low>对，就好像还有别的事情想弄清楚。我不知道。我越是思考为什么我会对此有这种感觉，就越有一种奇怪的顿悟：“噢，我觉得你们是在问，在我们的关系里谁是‘男人’。”

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:33.660
<v Tobin Low>[LAUGHTER]
<v Tobin Low>[笑声]

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:36.760
<v Ira  Glass>Right. You're both men.
<v Ira  Glass>没错。你们都是男人。

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:37.440
<v Tobin Low>Yes.
<v Tobin Low>是的。

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:39.840
<v Ira Glass>But one of you is really the man.
<v Ira Glass>但你们中有一个才是真正的“男人”。

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.840
<v Tobin Low>Yes. Yeah.
<v Tobin Low>是的，没错。

00:01:44.840 --> 00:02:02.730
<v Ira  Glass>Then, when Tobin would tell them that it was his husband who was the handy one, he felt like he was just giving them ammunition to put a picture of their relationship that just bugged him, like they were being sized up into familiar categories. Which of you is the husband? Which of you is the wife?
<v Ira  Glass>后来，当托宾告诉他们是他丈夫比较手巧时，他觉得这反而给了他们借口，让他们能用一种令他反感的刻板印象来看待他们的关系，就好像他们被强行套进了熟悉的类别里：你们俩谁是丈夫？谁是妻子？

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:23.010
<v Tobin Low>Like, it was weird, because whenever they would ask it, I could feel myself getting defensive. I didn't want to give them that picture. And I think part of my defensiveness came from, I think-- well, oh, man, not to take us in a whole other direction. But if you spend any amount of time in the closet--
<v Tobin Low>这很奇怪，因为每当他们问起时，我就能感觉到自己变得很有防御心。我不想给他们那种印象。我觉得我的防御心部分源于……唉，不谈这个了，别把话题带向另一个方向。但如果你在衣柜里待过一段时间——

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:26.240
<v Ira Glass>"In the closet." For Tobin, that means middle school and high school.
<v Ira Glass>“在衣柜里。”对托宾来说，这意味着初中和高中的那段时光。

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:59.210
<v Tobin Low>I think you're afraid of being found out at all as being effeminate in any way. Like, I know for me, I was very conscious of if anyone could detect, quote unquote, you know, "feminine" traits about me, and then figure out if I was gay or not. And so I do think that myself and a lot of gay men carry that around for kind of the rest of your life. And so I think that comes up in having to answer a question like this also.
<v Tobin Low>我觉得你会害怕被发现有任何女性化的一面。我知道我自己当时非常在意是否有人能察觉到我身上所谓的“女性”特质，从而判断我是不是同性恋。所以我认为，我和许多男同性恋者在余生中都背负着这种心理阴影。因此，我觉得当被问到这种问题时，这种心态就会表现出来。

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:08.820
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. Yeah, it's funny, because it's like this innocent question, and then really, underneath it, it's like there's a bomb waiting to go off, actually. Like, there's so many feelings.
<v Ira Glass>是啊。有趣的是，这听起来像是个无心的问题，但实际上，问题下面埋着一颗随时会爆炸的炸弹。真的，里面包含了太多的情绪。

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:20.440
<v Tobin Low>Yeah, it feels like it hits on a thing, at least for me, that I spent a lot of time as a kid running from, or spent a lot of time trying to not have to answer.
<v Tobin Low>是的，至少对我来说，这触及了我在孩童时期花费大量时间逃避或努力避免回答的问题。

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:21.580
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是啊。

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:32.350
<v Tobin Low>Like, how masculine am I? And is somebody else more masculine than I am?
<v Tobin Low>比如：我有多男性化？以及，是否有人比我更男性化？

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:42.370
<v Tobin Low>And, like, I do want to make room for the idea that they could have meant none of this, like, absolutely none of this.
<v Tobin Low>而且，我确实想给他们留有余地，也许他们本意并非如此，完全不是那个意思。

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:44.450
<v Ira Glass>Did you address it directly with any of them?
<v Ira Glass>你有没有直接和他们谈过这个问题？

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:49.170
<v Tobin Low>No, because that would be bonkers. [LAUGHS] To just say--
<v Tobin Low>没有，因为那太疯狂了。[笑声]直接说——

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:49.780
<v Tobin Low>[LAUGHTER]
<v Tobin Low>[笑声]

00:03:49.780 --> 00:04:00.460
<v Tobin Low>Oh, you asked me who's handy. You're trying to say I'm not a man. Like, that-- the leap in logic to say that outright is so huge.
<v Tobin Low>“噢，你问我谁手巧，你是在试图说我不是个男人。”这种逻辑跳跃太大了，根本无法直说。

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:24.680
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our program, questions that contain other secret questions inside of them, questions that are wolves in sheep's clothing, and all kinds of situations that we've all been in-- in dating, in talking to strangers, in dealing with the saddest things that ever happened to us, and more. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>好了，今天我们的节目要讨论那些包含着另一个秘密问题的问题，那些披着羊皮的狼式问题，以及我们都经历过的各种情况——在约会中、与陌生人交谈时、处理生命中最悲伤的事情时，等等。来自WBEZ芝加哥，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:41.630
<v Ira Glass>OK, so instead of four different acts today, what we're going to do is we're going to present the show as four questions. Here's the first one. Question, Tell Me How You Feel About This.
<v Ira Glass>好了，今天我们不打算分四个部分，而是将节目呈现为四个问题。这是第一个问题：告诉我你对此的感受。

00:04:41.630 --> 00:05:00.020
<v Ira Glass>So Tobin, who you just heard, is one of the editors here at our show. And really, the idea for today's program came out of a conversation that happened at a staff meeting. And what happened was, we all got talking about these kind of question traps, where it seems like somebody is asking about one thing, but the question is a proxy for trying to figure out something else. Tobin will explain more.
<v Ira Glass>刚才你们听到的托宾，是我们节目组的编辑之一。实际上，今天节目的创意来自一次员工会议上的谈话。当时我们都在聊那种“问题陷阱”，即表面上看起来是在问一件事，但这个问题实际上是一个代理，目的是为了搞清楚别的事情。托宾会详细解释。

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:21.210
<v Tobin Low>The conversation was about the questions people ask on first dates, the kind that force who someone really is out into the open, maybe even without them realizing. One such question, I didn't even know was a thing. But a few of my co-workers said that for Black women of a certain age, it's having a kind of renaissance. Emanuele, our executive editor, asks it this way.
<v Tobin Low>那场对话是关于人们在第一次约会时会问的问题，那些能强行让对方展现真面目的问题，甚至连他们自己都没意识到。其中有一个问题，我以前根本不知道还有这种操作。但我的几位同事说，对于某个年龄段的黑人女性来说，这个问题正在“复兴”。埃曼纽尔，我们的执行编辑，是这样问的：

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:36.430
<v Emanuele Berry>"What do you think of Beyoncé?" It was a question that I found myself trying to ask, basically, a lot on first dates. Because it told me a lot about them. It's a question that tells you, one, in some ways, how they feel about a powerful Black woman.
<v Emanuele Berry>“你觉得碧昂丝怎么样？”在第一次约会时，我发现自己总是不自觉地想问这个问题。因为它能告诉我的信息太多了。这是一个能反映出对方在某些方面如何看待一位强大的黑人女性的问题。

00:05:36.430 --> 00:05:55.500
<v Emanuele Berry>It's a question that tells you how they think about Black women in general, kind of, a little bit, to me. And that if you feel the need to put her down or say something negative about her, it's like a real turnoff. It's like a red flag, basically.
<v Emanuele Berry>对我来说，这个问题在一定程度上也能反映出他们如何看待整体的黑人女性。如果你觉得有必要贬低她或者说她的坏话，那对我来说绝对是个“劝退点”。基本上就是一个红灯信号。

00:05:55.500 --> 00:06:01.510
<v Bim Adewunmi>If they describe her singing style as kind of like caterwauling or like, ugh, she's just screeching--
<v Bim Adewunmi>如果他们把她的演唱风格描述为像是“嚎叫”或者“噢，她只是在尖叫”——

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:05.480
<v Tobin Low>Bim, another producer, has also asked this question on many a date.
<v Tobin Low>比姆，另一位制作人，也在很多次约会中问过这个问题。

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:16.670
<v Bim Adewunmi>All these words that have double meanings if you're a woman, and also if you're a Black woman-- I'm just like, all right, so you don't like loud people. OK. OK. That means you don't like me.
<v Bim Adewunmi>所有这些词汇，如果你是女性，特别是黑人女性，它们都有双重含义。我只会觉得：好吧，也就是说你不喜欢声音大的人。好的。OK，这意味着你也不喜欢我。

00:06:16.670 --> 00:06:25.370
<v Tobin Low>Could you tell me about some specific times that you've asked the Beyoncé question, and what the guy's response was and what it told you?
<v Tobin Low>你能跟我讲讲你问过“碧昂丝问题”的具体几次经历吗？男方的反应是什么？这又向你传达了什么？

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:34.790
<v Emanuele Berry>First date, bar date, pretty standard. Beyoncé actually came on in the bar in the background. I was like, oh, What do you think about Beyoncé?
<v Emanuele Berry>第一次约会，在酒吧，很标准。背景音乐正好播到了碧昂丝的歌。我就问：“噢，你觉得碧昂丝怎么样？”

00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:50.240
<v Emanuele Berry>And he was like, I don't-- I don't understand what the big deal is about her. Like, you-- like, women act like they're in a cult or something. And it's like, they seem, like, crazy. I was like, oh, well, I really like her, and like, I don't really think I'm in a cult.
<v Emanuele Berry>他说：“我不……我不明白她有什么大不了的。女性的表现就像她们在一个邪教里一样。看起来很疯狂。”我说：“噢，我真的很喜欢她，而且我不觉得我是在什么邪教里。”

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:56.200
<v Tobin  Low>Emanuele walks the guy through all the reasons Beyoncé is, in fact, pretty great. But the guy didn't budge.
<v Tobin  Low>埃曼纽尔给对方列举了碧昂丝确实非常出色的所有理由。但对方完全不为所动。

00:06:56.200 --> 00:07:04.970
<v Emanuele Berry>No, he did not-- didn't care to. And maybe he didn't care to hear me talk in general, is what it seemed like. (LAUGHING) So.
<v Emanuele Berry>没有，他根本不关心。而且看起来他甚至不关心听我说话。（笑声）所以。

00:07:04.970 --> 00:07:25.200
<v Tobin Low>They did not go out again, because, well, you could say he was unapologetic when he fucked up the night. That's a play on a Beyoncé lyric, by the way. Sorry, couldn't help myself. Anyway, BA Parker, who's also been on the show-- she said, for her, it doesn't even have to be Beyoncé. Any well-known Black woman does the trick.
<v Tobin Low>他们没有第二次约会，因为，嗯，你可以说他在搞砸那个夜晚时毫无愧疚。顺便说一句，这是对碧昂丝歌词的引用。抱歉，我忍不住。总之，也是本节目常客的BA·帕克说，对她而言，甚至不需要非得是碧昂丝，任何知名的黑人女性都可以达到目的。

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:56.050
<v BA Parker>If you say Serena Williams, and they say, oh, I think she's overrated, or if you say, Jada Pinkett, it's like, oh, she's too masculine, or she's ruining Will Smith's life, she's controlling him-- or How do you feel about Lizzo? Like, she needs to cover her ass. I bring up a Black female celebrity to get their opinion on them, and it usually becomes like the litmus test for how they would treat me as a partner, how they would view me as a person.
<v BA Parker>如果你提到塞雷娜·威廉姆斯，他们说“噢，我觉得她被高估了”；或者提到贾达·平克特，他们说“噢，她太男性化了”或者“她毁了威尔·史密斯的生活，她在控制他”；或者“你觉得利佐怎么样？”比如“她需要遮住屁股”。我会提起一位黑人女名流来获取他们的看法，这通常成了他们会如何对待我作为伴侣、如何看待我作为一个人的试金石。

00:07:56.050 --> 00:08:16.030
<v Tobin Low>But the Beyoncé question, she agrees, is the most potent. Because the answer can really tell you if you should be crazy in love or putting everything he owns in a box to the left. Again, I am so sorry. The thing about a bunch of people using the same trick, though, is that eventually, people-- in this case, men-- might catch on.
<v Tobin Low>但她也同意，“碧昂丝问题”是最有效的。因为答案真的能告诉你，你是该坠入爱河，还是该把他所有家当装进箱子里扔出去。再次重申，我非常抱歉。不过，很多人使用同样的套路，久而久之，人们——在这种情况下，是男人们——可能会察觉到。

00:08:16.030 --> 00:08:18.150
<v Tobin Low>Are you aware of the Beyoncé question?
<v Tobin Low>你知道“碧昂丝问题”吗？

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:20.940
<v Emmanuel  Dzotsi>Yes, I am aware of the Beyoncé question.
<v Emmanuel  Dzotsi>是的，我知道。

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:24.100
<v Tobin Low>Emmanuel Dzotsi, producer and man at the show--
<v Tobin Low>埃曼纽尔·佐齐，制作人，本节目的男性成员——

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:25.840
<v Tobin Low>Have you experienced this?
<v Tobin Low>你经历过吗？

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:28.320
<v Emmanuel  Dzotsi>Yes, I've experienced it many times.
<v Emmanuel  Dzotsi>是的，我经历过很多次。

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:45.340
<v Tobin Low>He told me about a date he was on where they started talking about musicals, and the movie version of Dreamgirls came up. And thinking he was just answering a question about the movie, Emmanuel was honest. He said, Beyoncé was just OK in that. He didn't realize he was answering the wrong question.
<v Tobin Low>他告诉我一次约会经历，他们开始聊音乐剧，谈到了电影版《追梦女郎》。埃曼纽尔以为自己只是在回答关于电影的问题，就老实回答了。他说：“碧昂丝在里面演得一般。”他当时根本没意识到自己是在回答一个错误的问题。

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:51.670
<v Emmanuel  Dzotsi>Um, and I was just digging a hole. [LAUGHS]
<v Emmanuel  Dzotsi>嗯，我当时简直是在自掘坟墓。[笑声]

00:08:51.670 --> 00:08:58.330
<v Emmanuel  Dzotsi>She was just like, the only answer to being asked about Beyoncé is that, yes, she's fantastic. She's amazing. Nobody can do what she does.
<v Emmanuel  Dzotsi>她的意思是，当被问到碧昂丝时，唯一的答案就是：是的，她太棒了。她很惊艳。没有人能像她那样。

00:08:58.330 --> 00:09:04.700
<v Tobin Low>It was only later that he learned from another guy friend why he, as a Black man, should really only answer one way.
<v Tobin Low>直到后来，他从另一位男性朋友那里才明白，作为黑人男性，为什么他其实只能有一种回答。

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:21.550
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>I remember my friend saying, basically, that is the question Black women will ask you to determine if you really like Black women. Once it was explained to me, like, I totally understood where people were coming from. And I understood what the purpose of that question was.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>我记得我朋友告诉我，基本上，这就是黑人女性会问你的问题，用来判断你是否真心喜欢黑人女性。一旦解释清楚，我完全理解了她们的出发点。我也明白了那个问题的目的。

00:09:21.550 --> 00:09:34.760
<v Tobin  Low>In some cases, the Beyoncé question is like an agreed-upon farce, where both parties know they're talking in code. Parker was recently on a date. She mentioned Beyoncé's Black is King film.
<v Tobin  Low>在某些情况下，“碧昂丝问题”就像是一场心照不宣的闹剧，双方都知道他们是在用暗语交谈。帕克最近去约会，她提到了碧昂丝的电影《黑人是国王》。

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:52.460
<v BA Parker>And he was like-- uh, (LAUGHING) He was like, I don't know what to say here. 'Cause I-- I-- I like this. I like talking to you, but I don't love Beyoncé. (LAUGHING) And I don't want you to be mad at me.
<v BA Parker>他说——呃，（笑声）他说：“我不知道该说什么。因为我……我喜欢这个。我喜欢和你聊天，但我并不爱碧昂丝。（笑声）我不希望你生我的气。”

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:54.740
<v Tobin Low>He knew it was a trap.
<v Tobin Low>他知道这是一个陷阱。

00:09:54.740 --> 00:09:57.310
<v BA Parker>(LAUGHING) He did know it was a trap.
<v BA Parker>（笑声）他确实知道是陷阱。

00:09:57.310 --> 00:10:00.720
<v Tobin  Low>And how did you respond?
<v Tobin  Low>那你怎么回应的？

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:11.980
<v BA Parker>I was like, What are you talking about? What are you-- And he was like, I know girls do this. And I was like, you're-- you're right. And I'm sorry.
<v BA Parker>我说：“你在说什么啊？你……”他说：“我知道女生会这样。”我说：“你是……你是对的。我道歉。”

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:21.430
<v BA Parker>(LAUGHING) I apologized. And I was like, well, I guess that's kind of the right answer. So we had a couple more dates.
<v BA Parker>（笑声）我道歉了。我说：“好吧，我想这确实是正确的答案。”所以我们又约会了几次。

00:10:21.430 --> 00:10:48.400
<v Tobin    Low>Of course, there are other questions like the Beyoncé one-- little traps we set on dates, hoping the other person doesn't fall in, or hoping they do. One that made the news recently, which may or may not be true-- according to an old classmate, Governor Ron DeSantis would ask dates if they liked Thai food. But-- and this is key-- he'd pronounce it "Thigh" food.
<v Tobin    Low>当然，还有其他像“碧昂丝问题”一样的陷阱——我们在约会时设下的小陷阱，希望对方不要掉进去，或者希望他们掉进去。最近上新闻的一个例子——是真是假不确定——据一位老同学说，罗恩·德桑蒂斯州长会问约会对象是否喜欢泰国菜（Thai food）。但是——关键点在于——他会把它读成“大腿菜”（Thigh food）。

00:10:48.400 --> 00:11:01.180
<v Tobin    Low>And if they said, no, it's Thai food, not "thigh," he'd ditch the date. It was his way of testing if they'd correct him, which he did not want. I don't know. It sounds like a test I'd be grateful to fail, but that's just me.
<v Tobin    Low>如果她们说：“不对，是Thai（泰国），不是Thigh（大腿）”，他就会中止约会。这是他测试对方是否会纠正他的方式，而他并不想被纠正。我不知道。这听起来像是一个我很高兴能挂科的测试，但这只是我的看法。

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:21.320
<v Tobin    Low>Anyway, I talked to a bunch of other people about their question traps. Kelsey in Minnesota asked her dates about their favorite Tom Hanks movies. She said he's been in so many movies across multiple genres, the answer is kind of like a personality test. Toy Story, for example, tells her there is a stunted-adolescence thing going on.
<v Tobin    Low>总之，我跟其他人聊了聊他们的“陷阱问题”。明尼苏达州的凯尔西会问约会对象他们最喜欢的汤姆·汉克斯电影。她说他演过那么多不同类型的电影，这个答案简直就像是一场人格测试。比如《玩具总动员》，就会告诉她对方有一种停滞不前的青少年心理。

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:38.700
<v Tobin    Low>Sarah in Tampa said when she started to get a weird vibe, she'd ask, What's your favorite conspiracy theory? Most people would keep their answers lighthearted, but occasionally, someone would go all in. One guy started talking all about Nazi separatists. She's Jewish, so you know, kind of a deal breaker.
<v Tobin    Low>坦帕市的萨拉说，当她开始感到不对劲时，她会问：“你最喜欢的阴谋论是什么？”大多数人会轻松作答，但偶尔有人会全盘托出。有个人开始谈论纳粹分裂主义。她是犹太人，所以你们懂的，这直接谈崩了。

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:53.270
<v Tobin    Low>But not all question traps are subtle. There's another genre that I was surprised anyone fell for-- the kind of question that seemed covered in yellow caution tape and a sign that said, this is a trap. This one comes from Vivian in Iowa.
<v Tobin    Low>但并非所有的陷阱问题都很微妙。还有另一种类型，我惊讶居然有人会上钩——那种看起来贴满了黄色警戒线、写着“这是陷阱”标志的问题。这个问题来自爱荷华州的维维安。

00:11:53.270 --> 00:12:09.390
<v Tobin    Low>After her husband died in 2016, she found herself back out on the dating scene. Her question on a date was, If your ex walked by right now with their new partner, what would you do? Which was her way of asking a much more interesting question-- How fucked up was your last relationship?
<v Tobin    Low>她丈夫在2016年去世后，她重返约会市场。她在约会时的问题是：“如果你的前任现在带着新伴侣路过，你会怎么做？”这是她用来询问一个更有意思的问题的方法——“你的上一段感情有多烂？”

00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:14.220
<v Vivian>First time I did it, the guy said, I would punch him and give her a piece of my mind.
<v Vivian>第一次问的时候，对方说：“我会打他一拳，然后让她听听我的意见。”

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:15.960
<v Tobin  Low>Oh, my god.
<v Tobin  Low>我的天。

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:38.760
<v Vivian>(LAUGHING) Exactly. We had just sat down to have a nice lunch on a Sunday afternoon. So I'm like, do I get up and go, or-- [LAUGHS] And that's when the story came out of how he was still about a couple of weeks away from going to court for finalizing his divorce.
<v Vivian>（笑声）没错。我们当时才刚坐下来，想在一个周日下午享受一顿午餐。所以我当时想，我是该起身走人，还是……[笑声]就在那时，他讲出了他当时离法院正式判决离婚只差几周的事。

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:57.150
<v Vivian>And it had been a 38-year relationship, and he found out she had been cheating for most of the time, in a completely serendipitous way. She gave him an old phone that she had wiped, and when she downloaded the cloud, it downloaded into his phone, too. And that's how he found out.
<v Vivian>那是一段38年的关系，他发现她大部分时间都在出轨，而且发现的方式非常偶然。她给了他一部她清空过的旧手机，结果当她同步云端时，信息也同步到了他的手机上。就是这样发现的。

00:12:57.150 --> 00:13:01.210
<v Tobin  Low>Wow, you got so much information from that one question.
<v Tobin  Low>哇，你通过这一个问题得到了这么多信息。

00:13:01.210 --> 00:13:05.390
<v Vivian>You got to make it efficient. Why draw it out?
<v Vivian>必须得高效。为什么要拖泥带水？

00:13:05.390 --> 00:13:17.650
<v Tobin Low>I was shocked. This question is so clearly, How bad was your last breakup? Do I have anything to worry about? But something about turning it into a fun little icebreaker made these guys open up.
<v Tobin Low>我当时惊呆了。这个问题显然就是在问：“你的上一段感情有多糟糕？我有什么好担心的吗？”但不知为何，把它变成一个有趣的破冰话题，让这些男人们敞开了心扉。

00:13:17.650 --> 00:13:28.090
<v Vivian>There was another guy that said, well, we would have to leave immediately, because I don't want to see them. And my reply was, You don't want to see them, or you don't want them to see us?
<v Vivian>还有另一个人说：“嗯，我们必须立刻离开，因为我不想见到他们。”我的回答是：“你是不想见到他们，还是不想让他们见到我们？”

00:13:28.090 --> 00:13:30.000
<v Tobin Low>And what was his response?
<v Tobin Low>他怎么回应的？

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:39.250
<v Vivian>Oh, he-- never answered directly. But I knew then that he was still in a relationship.
<v Vivian>噢，他……从未直接回答。但我那时就知道他还在纠缠不清。

00:13:39.250 --> 00:13:48.910
<v Tobin Low>With her now-husband, they met at a widows' support group. He talked about his loneliness and being a single parent. They kind of just got each other.
<v Tobin Low>至于她现在的丈夫，他们是在丧偶互助会上认识的。他谈到了自己的孤独和单身父亲的生活。他们就是那种心有灵犀的感觉。

00:13:48.910 --> 00:14:02.930
<v Tobin Low>And she knew the question, What would you do if your ex showed up? would not be right for this nice guy who had just lost his wife. She wasn't going to ask that.
<v Tobin Low>她知道“如果前任出现你会怎么做？”这个问题，对于这个刚刚失去了妻子的好男人来说是不合适的。她不会问那个问题。

00:14:02.930 --> 00:14:07.320
<v Tobin Low>The last person I talked to was Jessica. She teaches ESL classes in Atlanta.
<v Tobin Low>我聊的最后一个人是杰西卡。她在亚特兰大教英语。

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:10.160
<v Tobin Low>What's your go-to question?
<v Tobin Low>你的必杀技问题是什么？

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:12.800
<v Jessica>Uh, "Do you believe in ghosts?"
<v Jessica>呃，“你相信有鬼吗？”

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:20.800
<v Tobin Low>Ghosts. I bet you didn't see that one coming, did you-- "Do you believe in ghosts?" Here's how Jessica says it works.
<v Tobin Low>有鬼。我敢打赌你没料到这个，对吧——“你相信有鬼吗？”杰西卡是这样解释的。

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:29.120
<v Jessica>There is no one right answer. It just matters that you and your partner have the same answer, essentially, at its core. Your minds kind of work in a similar way.
<v Jessica>没有唯一正确的答案。重要的是你和伴侣从根本上拥有相同的答案。你们的思想以相似的方式运作。

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:43.140
<v Tobin  Low>Was there ever a time that you asked the ghost question, the person answered differently than you, and you went ahead and dated that person anyway? And how did that go?
<v Tobin  Low>有没有过你问了“鬼魂问题”，对方的回答和你不同，但你还是继续跟他约会了？结果怎么样？

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:54.760
<v Jessica>[CHUCKLES] Yes. Yeah. I was engaged before I married my husband now. And the ghost question really should have been my get-the-fuck-out moment. [LAUGHS]
<v Jessica>[笑声]有。是的。我和现在丈夫结婚前订过婚。那个“鬼魂问题”真的应该成为我赶紧分手的信号。[笑声]

00:14:54.760 --> 00:14:56.010
<v Tobin Low>Really?
<v Tobin Low>真的吗？

00:14:56.010 --> 00:14:57.260
<v Jessica>Yeah.
<v Jessica>是啊。

00:14:57.260 --> 00:15:06.130
<v Tobin Low>Her answer to the question is, I don't really believe in ghosts, but if there was evidence to the contrary, I could be convinced. I'm open to changing my mind.
<v Tobin Low>她对那个问题的回答是：“我不怎么相信有鬼，但如果有相反的证据，我可以被说服。我乐于改变自己的看法。”

00:15:06.130 --> 00:15:17.080
<v Jessica>And his response was, no, and there is no information that you could give me to change my mind. And I just-- I don't see why anyone would really think that.
<v Jessica>而他的回应是：“不，没有任何信息能让我改变看法。我实在不明白为什么有人会那么想。”

00:15:17.080 --> 00:15:25.000
<v Tobin Low>At the time, she didn't think much about the difference in their answers. But then, as she got to know him better, other things would come up.
<v Tobin Low>当时，她没怎么在意他们答案的差异。但后来，当她更了解他时，其他问题也出现了。

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:37.040
<v Jessica>And I was frustrated about the fact that, like, everything with you is so black-and-white. Like, not everything is black-and-white. Sometimes they're gray. And then I thought back to his answer to this question.
<v Jessica>我对他那种凡事非黑即白的态度感到沮丧。并不是所有事情都是黑白分明的。有时是灰色的。然后我想起了他对这个问题的回答。

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:54.870
<v Tobin Low>His rigidity was one of the big things that broke them up. Now, she tells everyone she knows, if you're seeing someone new, ask them the ghost question. It could save you a lot of time.
<v Tobin Low>他的僵化是导致他们分手的主要原因之一。现在，她告诉每个认识的人：如果你在和新人约会，就问他们“鬼魂问题”。这能为你节省很多时间。

00:15:54.870 --> 00:16:04.850
<v Tobin Low>The thing about any trap, of course, is there are ways to sidestep it, disarm it. And then, the person who laid the trap has to decide what to do. Emanuele had to make such a decision.
<v Tobin Low>关于任何陷阱，当然，都有规避或化解的方法。然后，设下陷阱的人就得决定该怎么做。埃曼纽尔就必须做出这样的决定。

00:16:04.850 --> 00:16:13.080
<v Emanuele Berry>Ironically enough, my current boyfriend had no idea who Beyoncé was-- who's the only person who had that response.
<v Emanuele Berry>讽刺的是，我现在的男朋友根本不知道碧昂丝是谁——他是唯一给出这种反应的人。

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:14.770
<v Tobin Low>What did that tell you?
<v Tobin Low>这告诉了你什么？

00:16:14.770 --> 00:16:18.210
<v Emanuele Berry>That he just needed, like, some education?
<v Emanuele Berry>他只是需要一点点“教育”？

00:16:18.210 --> 00:16:18.810
<v Emanuele Berry>[LAUGHTER]
<v Emanuele Berry>[笑声]

00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:33.910
<v Emanuele Berry>He's older, and he's not from this country, and doesn't listen to music, really, and like, by the second date, he had read the entire Wikipedia page for me, and he knew Beyoncé's birthday, and that she was married to Jay-Z, and he knew about the elevator fight.
<v Emanuele Berry>他年纪大一点，不是这个国家的人，也不怎么听音乐。到第二次约会时，他已经帮我读完了整页维基百科，他知道碧昂丝的生日，知道她和Jay-Z结婚了，还知道电梯斗殴事件。

00:16:33.910 --> 00:16:36.030
<v Tobin Low>So he listened to your opinion.
<v Tobin Low>所以他听进了你的看法。

00:16:36.030 --> 00:16:52.490
<v Emanuele Berry>Yeah. It ended up being a green flag. Now, I talk to him about Beyoncé all the time, and I don't necessarily think he's, like-- he's not going out to Beyoncé concerts or anything, but he understands how important she is and how important she is to me, and lets me rant about her, so, that's all you ever really want, right?
<v Emanuele Berry>是啊。这最后成了一个“绿灯”信号。现在，我总是跟他聊碧昂丝，我不一定认为他会……他不会去碧昂丝的演唱会什么的，但他理解碧昂丝对我的重要性，也允许我发牢骚，所以，这不就是你真正想要的吗？

00:16:52.490 --> 00:17:11.800
<v Tobin  Low>It is. All I think anyone wants is someone you don't feel like you have to set a trap for, someone who you can look at them and say, you're everything I need and more. It's written all over your face. Baby, I can feel your halo. Pray it won't fade away. Sorry, I'm going to stop now.
<v Tobin  Low>确实。我想每个人想要的只是一个不需要你费心设下陷阱的人，一个看着他就能说：“你就是我需要的一切，甚至更多。”这都写在你的脸上了。宝贝，我能感受到你的光环。祈祷它永远不会褪色。抱歉，我要停下了。

00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:16.260
<v Ira Glass>Tobin Low is an editor on our program.
<v Ira Glass>托宾·洛是我们节目的编辑。

00:17:16.260 --> 00:17:38.410
<v Ira Glass>Question Two, How Old Are Your Kids? So there's a particular piece of small talk that happens all the time that, for some people, is the most normal thing in the world, and for others, is a super delicate minefield.
<v Ira Glass>第二个问题：你的孩子多大了？有些闲聊的话题对某些人来说是最自然不过的事，但对另一些人来说，却是极其微妙的雷区。

00:17:38.410 --> 00:17:53.740
<v Ira Glass>This story that you're about to hear is about a couple for whom it is a minefield. And how, one day, a question like this comes up, and it goes completely differently from how it's ever gone before for them, in a spectacularly wild way. You'll hear what I'm talking about. Chris Benderev tells the story.
<v Ira Glass>你即将听到的故事，是关于一对将此视为雷区的夫妻。以及有一天，当这样一个问题出现时，它与他们过去经历的任何情况完全不同，且以一种极其疯狂的方式发生了。你会明白我在说什么。克里斯·本德雷夫讲述了这个故事。

00:17:53.740 --> 00:18:15.370
<v Chris Benderev>Stacey Silberman is a real estate agent in Southern California, and she's well suited to the job because she's excellent at making conversation with strangers about anything. But for the past six and a half years, there's been this classic genre of get-to-know-you banter that's become a lot more complicated for her and her husband, Michael. And that's questions about their children.
<v Chris Benderev>斯泰西·希尔伯曼是南加州的一名房地产经纪人，她很适合这份工作，因为她非常擅长与陌生人谈论任何话题。但在过去的六年半里，有一种经典的交友闲聊对她和丈夫迈克尔来说变得复杂多了。那就是关于他们孩子的问题。

00:18:15.370 --> 00:18:24.590
<v Stacey>Um, yes, all the time. Talking to people, getting introductions-- it's always asking about our kids. How many kids do you have? Blah, blah, blah, blah. How old are they?
<v Stacey>嗯，是的，总是这样。和人聊天、被介绍认识——总是会问到我们的孩子。“你有几个孩子？等等等等。他们多大了？”

00:18:24.590 --> 00:18:30.960
<v Chris Benderev>The answers to these questions are complicated for Stacey, because her older child, Max, died in 2017.
<v Chris Benderev>回答这些问题对斯泰西来说很复杂，因为她的大孩子，马克斯，在2017年去世了。

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:36.060
<v Michael>So it has been six and a half years, because I have a dot tattoo for every six months.
<v Michael>所以已经六年半了，因为我每六个月就会纹一个小点点。

00:18:36.060 --> 00:18:43.660
<v Chris Benderev>This is Stacey's husband, Michael, Max's dad. He's a CPA, straight-laced kind of guy, for the most part.
<v Chris Benderev>这是斯泰西的丈夫迈克尔，马克斯的爸爸。他是个注册会计师，大部分时候是个循规蹈矩的人。

00:18:43.660 --> 00:18:47.310
<v Michael>Max always wanted us to get tattoos, and we never did. And I feel like--
<v Michael>马克斯一直想让我们纹身，但我们从未尝试过。我觉得——

00:18:47.310 --> 00:18:48.820
<v Chris Benderev>I heard he had some. He had some.
<v Chris Benderev>我听说他有一些。他有一些。

00:18:48.820 --> 00:19:01.140
<v Michael>He had a wonderful sleeve and lots of tattoos. But-- so I'm covered in tattoos, and my right arm is an entire memorial for Maxie. I've got the kids tattooed up here on my right shoulder, so.
<v Michael>他有一个很棒的袖套纹身，身上还有很多纹身。但……所以我全身都是纹身，我的右手臂是为了纪念马克斯的。我的右肩上纹着孩子们，所以。

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:03.940
<v Chris Benderev>Did you have tattoos before he died?
<v Chris Benderev>你在他去世前有纹身吗？

00:19:03.940 --> 00:19:20.240
<v Michael>Never. But you know, it's-- tattoos are a very interesting thing. It's less than the least I can do, but it does help me through the pain, and tattooing really, how I see it from my perspective, is just socially acceptable cutting.
<v Michael>从来没有。但你知道，纹身是件很有意思的事。这微不足道，但确实能帮我熬过痛苦，而纹身，在我看来，只是社会可接受的自残方式。

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:32.880
<v Chris Benderev>Max was a funny kid, always loved playing pranks, who, by 15, was struggling with drug addiction, going in and out of treatment. He overdosed when he was 25 in his parents' house.
<v Chris Benderev>马克斯是个有趣的孩子，总是喜欢恶作剧。15岁时，他陷入了毒瘾，反复接受治疗。他在25岁时在父母家中服药过量去世。

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:45.500
<v Chris Benderev>And after he died, for a while, Michael and Stacey were around friends and family who knew what had happened. So nobody asked those "Do you have kids" sort of questions. But then Stacey traveled to a conference in Albuquerque.
<v Chris Benderev>他去世后，有一段时间，迈克尔和斯泰西身边都是知道真相的朋友和家人。所以没人问那些“你有孩子吗”之类的问题。但后来斯泰西去阿尔伯克基参加了一个会议。

00:19:45.500 --> 00:19:55.680
<v Chris Benderev>She was sitting down for lunch next to a couple-- friendly, blonde woman and her husband. And they began asking Stacey where she was from and what she did-- and then, finally, those questions.
<v Chris Benderev>她午餐时坐在了一对夫妻旁边——友好的金发女郎和她的丈夫。他们开始问斯泰西来自哪里，从事什么工作——最后，问到了那些问题。

00:19:55.680 --> 00:20:08.620
<v Stacey>"Do you have kids?" "How old are they?" And the woman was, you know, kind of Southern, very sweet, very bubbly. And when people are like that with me, you know, I'm pretty open.
<v Stacey>“你有孩子吗？”“他们多大了？”那个女人很随和，很甜美，很有活力。当人们那样对我时，你知道，我通常很坦诚。

00:20:08.620 --> 00:20:19.340
<v Stacey>And so I felt like being authentic. And I told her, you know, one of my children died of an overdose two years ago. And now, I have one.
<v Stacey>所以我当时想表现得真实一点。我告诉她：“你知道，我的一个孩子两年前因吸毒过量去世了。现在，我还有一个孩子。”

00:20:19.340 --> 00:20:21.550
<v Chris Benderev>This did not go over well.
<v Chris Benderev>这下弄得局面很尴尬。

00:20:21.550 --> 00:20:36.550
<v Stacey>This woman and her husband-- it really upset them. They couldn't-- they just couldn't handle the conversation. I just saw this major pity face, with the open mouth and the, oh, OK.
<v Stacey>那个女人和她丈夫——他们真的很难过。他们处理不了这段对话。我看到她那种典型的怜悯表情，张着嘴，说着“噢，好的”。

00:20:36.550 --> 00:20:41.120
<v Chris Benderev>And does the conversation with them kind of stall out at that point, and then they kind of go?
<v Chris Benderev>那场对话是不是就在那一刻陷入了僵局，然后他们就离开了？

00:20:41.120 --> 00:20:44.740
<v Stacey>Totally stalled. They never talked to me again.
<v Stacey>完全卡住了。他们再也没有跟我说话。

00:20:44.740 --> 00:20:56.510
<v Chris  Benderev>Of course, over time, this happened again and again. Strangers would ask them these sorts of questions. And when they'd answer, it'd suck all the air out of the room, which made Michael especially uncomfortable.
<v Chris  Benderev>当然，随着时间的推移，这种情况发生了一次又一次。陌生人会问他们这类问题。而当他们回答时，会瞬间抽干房间里的空气，这让迈克尔特别不舒服。

00:20:56.510 --> 00:21:04.670
<v Chris  Benderev>He never liked sharing this stuff with strangers. He's more of a private person. But together, he and Stacey came up with a strategy for how to handle things.
<v Chris  Benderev>他从不喜欢跟陌生人分享这些。他是个更内向的人。但迈克尔和斯泰西一起商量出了一个应对策略。

00:21:04.670 --> 00:21:20.180
<v Michael>When we're out and about and the question comes up, we look at each other just a little, imperceptibly so, so nobody could really pick up what's going on. And then usually, Stacey will answer however she answers, and I support her unreservedly.
<v Michael>当我们外出时遇到这个问题，我们会微微对视一下，动作很小，没人能察觉发生了什么。通常斯泰西会随口回答，而我无条件支持她。

00:21:20.180 --> 00:21:29.140
<v Stacey>A lot of the times, I actually lie. We have two kids. This is their ages. Talk to you later. [CHUCKLES] And keep it short and sweet.
<v Stacey>很多时候，我其实是在撒谎。“我们有两个孩子。他们是这些年龄。回见。”[笑声]保持简短。

00:21:29.140 --> 00:21:39.720
<v Stacey>Because sometimes the white lie is better for that person. Because they're at a party, they're out having fun, and they definitely don't want to hear about your dead child.
<v Stacey>因为有时小谎言对那个人更好。因为他们是在派对上，正在玩得开心，他们绝对不想听到关于你去世孩子的消息。

00:21:39.720 --> 00:22:03.280
<v Chris Benderev>And so on they went, answering some questions about their kids and bobbing and weaving around others for six and a half years-- until this one day last November, when they got themselves into a situation that was very different from any that they'd been in before-- and very public.
<v Chris Benderev>于是他们就这样继续生活着，回答一些关于孩子的问题，同时巧妙地回避其他问题——直到去年十一月的那一天，他们陷入了一个与以往任何时候都不同的境地——而且是在极其公开的情况下。

00:22:03.280 --> 00:22:12.100
<v Chris Benderev>One thing to know about Stacey and Michael is they both spend a lot of their time working at a recovery center for people struggling with addiction. And sometimes they hang out with the other staff and clients there.
<v Chris Benderev>关于斯泰西和迈克尔，有一点需要知道，他们两人大部分时间都在一家针对成瘾者的戒毒中心工作。有时他们会和那里的其他员工和客户一起聚会。

00:22:12.100 --> 00:22:20.740
<v Stacey>And so I saw that they were going to a show at the Hollywood Improv where Sarah Silverman was performing, and I'm like, oh, my god, I love Sarah Silverman. I want to go. I want to go.
<v Stacey>我看到他们要去好莱坞即兴表演剧场看萨拉·西尔弗曼的表演，我就想：“天哪，我太爱萨拉·西尔弗曼了。我想去。我想去。”

00:22:20.740 --> 00:22:36.300
<v Stacey>And I said when we got there, I go, I want to sit front row, center. I want to get heckled. [CHUCKLES] I want to be right under Sarah Silverman. I want her to heckle me if possible. Or me-- I guess we heckle them. I don't know. I just wanted to be a part of it.
<v Stacey>到那儿时我说，我想坐在前排中间。我想被嘲讽。[笑声]我想坐在萨拉·西尔弗曼正下方。如果可能的话，我想让她嘲讽我。或者我们嘲讽他们。我不知道。我只是想成为其中的一部分。

00:22:36.300 --> 00:22:51.830
<v Chris Benderev>They get seats, front row center. But before Sarah Silverman came on stage that night at the Hollywood Improv, there was this opener-- guy named Adam Ray, early 40s, wearing a Mariners cap. And he's got this backing band-- drums, keyboard, backup singers.
<v Chris Benderev>他们坐在前排正中间。但在萨拉·西尔弗曼登台前，有个暖场表演者——一个名叫亚当·雷的家伙，40出头，戴着水手队的帽子。他有个伴奏乐队——鼓、键盘、伴唱。

