00:30:39.710 --> 00:31:03.700
<v Ira   Glass>Chris Benderev is one of the producers of our show. Coming up-- a question about a 400-year-old play and the personal question underneath that question. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira   Glass>克里斯·本德雷夫是我们节目的制作人之一。接下来——一个关于400年历史戏剧的问题，以及这个问题背后个人层面的疑问。稍后回来，芝加哥公共广播，当我们的节目继续。

00:31:03.700 --> 00:31:30.700
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program-- the question trap. What we're talking about today is those questions that can seem benevolent, innocent, harmless, innocuous, could not hurt a fly. But underneath, they're really asking something else, or quietly making a point about something else. We've arrived at question three of our program-- Question Three, How's Your Mom?
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目——“问题陷阱”。我们今天要聊的是那些看起来仁慈、天真、无害、天衣无缝的问题。但深入探究，它们其实是在问别的事情，或者在安静地强调其他观点。我们来到了节目的第三个问题：第三个问题，你妈妈怎么样？

00:31:30.700 --> 00:32:00.930
<v Ira Glass>So we spotted this next thing we want to play you in an academic journal. It was originally a paper in Medical Anthropology Quarterly, written by an anthropologist named Janelle Taylor, who adapted it to read here on the radio. This one question that Janelle Taylor is writing about-- it kept showing up all the time in her personal life. And she says, as an anthropologist, she knows, when lots of people are asking the same question over and over, it means something. And she wrote this essay to think through what is underneath that question.
<v Ira Glass>我们是在一份学术期刊上发现这个我们要播给你们听的内容的。它最初是《医学人类学季刊》上的一篇论文，由一位名叫珍妮尔·泰勒的人类学家撰写，她改编后在电台朗读。珍妮尔·泰勒所写的这个问题，一直在她个人生活中反复出现。她说，作为人类学家，她知道，当很多人一遍又一遍问同一个问题时，它一定意味着什么。她写下这篇文章是为了深思那个问题背后到底是什么。

00:32:00.930 --> 00:32:15.280
<v Janelle  Taylor>My mother is living with progressive dementia. Because I'm reading these words on the radio, I can't hear your response. But I'm listening for the question that, as I've learned, always comes.
<v Janelle  Taylor>我的母亲患有进行性痴呆。因为我是在电台上朗读这些文字，我听不到你们的回应。但我一直在等待那个我已学到、一定会来的问题。

00:32:15.280 --> 00:32:32.890
<v Janelle  Taylor>Everyone, almost without exception, responds with some version of the same question. Does she recognize you? There are variants, of course. Does she still know who you are? But does she still know your name?
<v Janelle  Taylor>每个人，几乎无一例外，都会用同一种问题的某种形式来回应。“她认得你吗？”当然有变体。比如“她还知道你是谁吗？”或者“她还记得你的名字吗？”

00:32:32.890 --> 00:33:08.870
<v Janelle  Taylor>However it may be phrased, the question is always whether my mother recognizes me, meaning, can she recite the facts of who I am, what my name is, and how I'm related to her? When everyone keeps asking me, Does she recognize you? I find myself thinking, that is the wrong question. I believe the question really is-- or should be-- Do you, do we, recognize her as a person who is still here?
<v Janelle  Taylor>无论怎么表述，问题总是关于我母亲是否认得我，意思是：她能说出关于我是谁、我叫什么名字、以及我们是什么关系的事实吗？当每个人都在问我“她认得你吗？”时，我发现自己想的是：那是个错误的问题。我相信真正的问题应该是——或者应该是——你、我们，是否还认出她作为一个依然存在的人？

00:33:08.870 --> 00:33:25.750
<v Janelle  Taylor>"Does she recognize you?" The weirdness of the question becomes more obvious if you think about what would be required to answer it. Let's say I asked my mother, What's my name? Who am I? How old am I? How do we know each other?
<v Janelle  Taylor>“她认得你吗？”如果你思考回答这个问题需要什么，这个问题的荒谬性就变得更加明显了。假设我问我母亲：“我叫什么名字？我是谁？我多大了？我们是怎么认识的？”

00:33:25.750 --> 00:33:40.880
<v Janelle  Taylor>Testing her that way-- what does it prove? What does it actually accomplish? I read a book by a journalist named Lauren Kessler. She wrote about how she would correct her own mother when her mother called her by the wrong name.
<v Janelle  Taylor>那样测试她——能证明什么？实际上完成了什么？我读过一位名叫劳伦·凯斯勒的记者的书。她写了关于她如何纠正自己的母亲，当母亲叫错她名字时。

00:33:40.880 --> 00:33:53.050
<v Janelle  Taylor>Every time she would visit her mother, she'd take framed photos from the dresser and point to them and quiz her mother. You know who this is, don't you, Mom? Of course, she didn't.
<v Janelle  Taylor>每次她去探望母亲，她都会从梳妆台上拿起有框的照片，指着它们测试母亲。“你知道这是谁，对吧，妈？”当然，她不知道。

00:33:53.050 --> 00:34:07.050
<v Janelle  Taylor>Kessler writes, "So I told her, again and again, each visit, who is who, and then quizzed again. Thinking back on this now, I am appalled at my insensitivity. What did I think I was doing?
<v Janelle  Taylor>凯斯勒写道：“所以我告诉她，一次又一次，每次探望都告诉她谁是谁，然后再测试。现在回想起来，我对自己当时的迟钝感到震惊。我以为自己在做什么？

00:34:07.050 --> 00:34:24.960
<v Janelle  Taylor>I managed to accomplish only two things. I made myself miserable, and I made my mother irritable. I don't need my mother to tell me my name or how I'm related to her. I already know these things, and I know that she has dementia.
<v Janelle  Taylor>我只做成了两件事。我让自己变得痛苦，也让我母亲变得烦躁。我不需要母亲告诉我我的名字或我们是什么关系。我已经知道这些了，我也知道她患有痴呆症。

00:34:24.960 --> 00:34:47.470
<v Janelle  Taylor>So why then would I make a point of asking her these questions that I know she can't possibly answer? It seems rude, or just mean. I can't bring myself to do it. I guess you could say that my mother raised me better than that."
<v Janelle  Taylor>那为什么我还要执着于问她那些我知道她绝不可能回答的问题呢？这看起来很粗鲁，或者只是刻薄。我无法强迫自己这样做。我想你可以说，我母亲把我教得比那好。”

00:34:47.470 --> 00:35:07.820
<v Janelle  Taylor>"Does she recognize you?" I'm not so convinced that the inability to remember names necessarily means that a person with dementia can't recognize or care about other people. But very often, it does mean that other people stop recognizing and caring about them.
<v Janelle  Taylor>“她认得你吗？”我并不确信记忆力衰退一定意味着痴呆症患者不能认出或关心其他人。但通常情况下，这确实意味着其他人停止了认出并关心他们。

00:35:07.820 --> 00:35:27.400
<v Janelle  Taylor>My mother was close to lots of people. But only one friend remains present in her life. Every month or two, Eli Davis drives an hour and a half from her home to Seattle to visit Mom, bringing treats and hugs and her always-cheerful self. I love her dearly for it.
<v Janelle  Taylor>我的母亲和很多人关系密切。但只有一个朋友依然存在于她的生活中。每隔一两个月，伊莱·戴维斯就会从她家开车一个半小时到西雅图探望妈妈，带着礼物、拥抱和她那一如既往快乐的自己。我非常爱她。

00:35:27.400 --> 00:35:48.480
<v Janelle  Taylor>And I wonder, where are the others? Where are the couples with whom my parents socialized, the women with whom Mom spent hours and hours on the phone all through my childhood? This shouldn't surprise me as much as it has. Maybe it's not fair to expect friends to step up. Even close family drop off.
<v Janelle  Taylor>我好奇，其他人呢？那些和我父母社交的夫妻，那些和妈妈在我整个童年时期通电话聊上几个小时的女人们呢？这本不该让我如此惊讶。也许指望朋友们挺身而出并不公平。即使是亲近的家人也会逐渐疏远。

00:35:48.480 --> 00:36:07.130
<v Janelle  Taylor>Friendships in America are not usually expected to survive dementia. Friendships are often more like pleasure crafts than life rafts-- not built to brave the really rough waters.
<v Janelle  Taylor>在美国，友谊通常不被期待能在痴呆症面前存活。友谊往往更像是休闲小船，而不是救生筏——不是为了度过大风大浪而建造的。

00:36:07.130 --> 00:36:26.550
<v Janelle  Taylor>"Does she recognize you?" When people ask me whether my mother still recognizes me, they're often expressing concern for me, asking me how I'm bearing up under the burden of suffering that her dementia must place on me. And they're quite ready to hear about my burdens and my suffering.
<v Janelle  Taylor>“她认得你吗？”当人们问我母亲是否还认得我时，他们通常是在表达对我的关心，问我如何承受痴呆症带给我的痛苦重担。他们非常乐于听到我的负担和痛苦。

00:36:26.550 --> 00:36:38.580
<v Janelle  Taylor>What they find harder to hear, I think, is that being around my mother is not a nightmare or a horror. It's not like any of that. Here's what it is.
<v Janelle  Taylor>我认为他们更难听到的是，陪伴我的母亲并不是一场噩梦或恐怖事件。根本不是那样的。情况是这样的：

00:36:38.580 --> 00:36:57.260
<v Janelle  Taylor>In a cafe, as we share a scone, Mom and I make what passes for conversation. I've learned to ask only the sort of question that doesn't require any specific information to answer. "So, things going OK with you these days?" "How's my favorite mom doing? You doing all right?"
<v Janelle  Taylor>在咖啡馆里，我们分享一块烤饼，妈妈和我进行着勉强算得上谈话的交流。我学会了只问那种不需要具体信息来回答的问题。“最近事情还顺利吗？”“我最爱的妈妈怎么样？你还好吗？”

00:36:57.260 --> 00:37:12.380
<v Janelle  Taylor>I tell her funny little stories about my kids. Sometimes we leaf through a magazine, looking at pictures and commenting on them. Sometimes we look out the window, and I make general observations that require no specific response.
<v Janelle  Taylor>我给她讲关于我孩子们的趣事。有时我们翻看杂志，看图评论。有时我们看着窗外，我做一些不需要具体回应的一般性观察。

00:37:12.380 --> 00:37:31.640
<v Janelle  Taylor>"Looks like spring is coming." "Look at those leaves coming out on the trees." "That guy's hair is really curly." With each exchange, Mom smiles at me, beaming affectionately in that familiar, slightly conspiratorial way, as if we're both in on the same joke.
<v Janelle  Taylor>“看起来春天要来了。”“看那些树上长出的叶子。”“那个人的头发真卷。”每一次交流，妈妈都对着我微笑，那种熟悉而略带同谋感的深情，仿佛我们俩都在分享同一个笑话。

00:37:31.640 --> 00:37:46.900
<v Janelle  Taylor>So our conversations go nowhere. But it doesn't matter what we say, really, or whether we said it before, or whether it's accurate or interesting or even comprehensible. The exchange is the point.
<v Janelle  Taylor>所以我们的对话哪里也没去。但我们说什么其实不重要，或者我们之前是否说过，或者它是否准确、有趣，甚至能否理解。交流本身就是重点。

00:37:46.900 --> 00:38:09.790
<v Janelle  Taylor>Mom and I are playing catch with touches, smiles, and gestures, as well as words, lobbing them back and forth to each other in slow, easy, underhand arcs. The fact that she drops the ball more and more often doesn't stop the game from being enjoyable. It's a way of being together.
<v Janelle  Taylor>妈妈和我在进行一场用触摸、微笑和手势，以及言语进行的接球游戏，将它们以缓慢、轻松、低手的弧线互相来回抛掷。她丢球的次数越来越多，并没有阻止这场游戏变得有趣。这是一种在一起的方式。

00:38:09.790 --> 00:38:28.780
<v Janelle  Taylor>"Does she recognize you?" She may not recognize me, in a narrowly cognitive sense. But my mom does recognize me as someone who's there with her, someone familiar, perhaps. And she doesn't need to have all the details sorted out in order to care for me.
<v Janelle  Taylor>“她认得你吗？”她在狭义的认知意义上可能不认得我。但我的妈妈确实认得我是一个和她在一起的人，或许是一个熟悉的人。她不需要搞清楚所有的细节来关心我。

00:38:28.780 --> 00:38:52.050
<v Janelle  Taylor>The impulse to care, the habit of caring-- these are things that run deep in my mother-- someone who, for most of her life, was very engaged in caring for other people-- her children, her husband, her grandchildren, her friends. Even some of the behavioral quirks that my mom has developed make sense to me in those terms-- as expressions of care.
<v Janelle  Taylor>关心的冲动，关心的习惯——这些在我母亲心中根深蒂固——她大半生都在非常投入地照顾别人——她的孩子们、丈夫、孙子们、朋友们。甚至我母亲发展出的一些行为怪癖，对我来说也能解释得通——作为关心的表达。

00:38:52.050 --> 00:39:10.880
<v Janelle  Taylor>Here's an example. People with dementia often engage in repetitive behaviors, and Mom is no exception. When I take her out to a cafe, I usually get a cup of black coffee for myself and order a cup of hot chocolate for her. Not too hot, and don't forget the whipped cream on top.
<v Janelle  Taylor>举个例子。痴呆症患者经常会进行重复性行为，妈妈也不例外。当我带她去咖啡馆时，我通常会给自己买杯黑咖啡，给她点杯热巧克力。不要太热，别忘了上面的鲜奶油。

00:39:10.880 --> 00:39:32.510
<v Janelle  Taylor>As we drink them, she checks constantly to see whether my cup and hers are even, whether the liquids have been drunk down to the same level. And if not, she'll hurry up and drink more to catch up, or else, stop and wait for me. Or if we share a cookie, she's concerned to make sure that the haves be the same size and that we eat them at the same rate.
<v Janelle  Taylor>当我们喝的时候，她会不停地检查我的杯子和她的杯子是否平衡，液体是否喝到了同样的高度。如果不平衡，她会赶快多喝点赶上，或者停下来等我。或者如果我们分享一块饼干，她会很担心，确保剩下的部分大小一样，且我们吃它们的速度是一样的。

00:39:32.510 --> 00:39:55.960
<v Janelle  Taylor>I think keeping track of whether our drinks and cookies are even comes naturally to my mother-- a woman who has always had to carefully divide quite limited resources-- first, with her own brothers, and later, among her four children. She's cared about such details all her life, and caring about them was also a way in which she cared for other people.
<v Janelle  Taylor>我觉得关注我们的饮料和饼干是否平衡是母亲的天性——她一生都必须小心翼翼地分配相当有限的资源——最初是和她自己的兄弟们，后来是在她四个孩子之间。她一生都在关注这些细节，关注这些也是她照顾别人的一种方式。

00:39:55.960 --> 00:40:18.940
<v Janelle  Taylor>Mom also does still take care of me, in some small but important ways. One time, a little more than a year ago, I stopped by the assisted living facility where she was living at the end of a very busy day in an especially hectic week. I had stayed up very late the night before, trying to finish grading student papers, then spent the whole day teaching and in meetings.
<v Janelle  Taylor>妈妈确实还在照顾我，以一些微小但重要的方式。有一次，大概一年多前，我在一个非常忙碌的星期结束时，顺路去探望她住的养老院。前一天晚上我熬得很晚，试图批改学生论文，然后整天都在教书和开会。

00:40:18.940 --> 00:40:29.290
<v Janelle  Taylor>I went with her up to her room. I turned on the TV, and we sat down together on the couch. I was exhausted. I leaned back and yawned.
<v Janelle  Taylor>我和她一起回到房间。我打开电视，我们在沙发上坐下。我精疲力竭。我靠在椅背上，打了个哈欠。

00:40:29.290 --> 00:40:48.460
<v Janelle  Taylor>Mom patted my hand and said to me, You're tired. Just go ahead and sleep. You can just lay down right here. And so I sat there next to my mom, holding her hand, feeling her warmth against me, all along one side of my body. And I leaned my head on her shoulder and slept.
<v Janelle  Taylor>妈妈拍了拍我的手，对我说：“你累了。去睡觉吧。你可以就躺在这里。”于是我坐在妈妈旁边，握着她的手，感受着她身体的温暖，就在我身体的一侧。我把头靠在她的肩膀上睡着了。

00:40:48.460 --> 00:41:20.060
<v Janelle  Taylor>"Does she recognize you?" For a while after we first moved my mother into an assisted living facility, she often said that she wanted to "go home." I understood this to mean that she wanted to move back to the house where she had lived for 40 years until my father's death, the house in which I grew up.
<v Janelle  Taylor>“她认得你吗？”在我们刚把母亲搬进养老院后的一段时间里，她经常说她想“回家”。我理解这指的是她想搬回父亲去世前她住了40年的那所房子，也就是我长大的那所房子。

00:41:20.060 --> 00:41:44.300
<v Janelle  Taylor>Usually, I responded with my own mild version of reality orientation, explaining as gently as possible that that house was all empty and cold now, and nobody was there to keep her company or help her do stuff, so it was probably better to stay here. One time, though, I asked her a question instead-- "You mean, home to the house up in Edmonds?"
<v Janelle  Taylor>通常，我用我温和的现实定向方式回应，尽可能温柔地解释那所房子现在空荡荡的，很冷，没有人在那儿陪她或帮她做事情，所以呆在这里可能更好。有一次，我却反问了她一个问题——“你是说，回埃德蒙兹的那所房子？”

00:41:44.300 --> 00:42:05.330
<v Janelle  Taylor>"No, on the farm," she answered. ''You go down--" With her raised arm, she traced out the curve of a long-ago road. For the first few years of her life, my mother had lived on a small farm in southern Idaho, before her father moved the family to Seattle during World War II to seek work on the docks.
<v Janelle  Taylor>“不，回农场，”她回答。“你去那儿——”她抬起手臂，划出了一条很久以前路的弧线。在我母亲生命的前几年，她住在爱达荷州南部的一个小农场里，后来二战期间，她父亲把全家搬到了西雅图，以便在码头找工作。

00:42:05.330 --> 00:42:21.450
<v Janelle  Taylor>"They're inside there," she added. "Who?" I asked. "My mom and my dad." My mother's in her 70s. Her parents are not waiting for her inside an Idaho farmhouse.
<v Janelle  Taylor>“他们就在里面，”她补充道。“谁？”我问。“我妈妈和我爸爸。”我母亲70多岁了。她的父母并没有在爱达荷州的农舍里等她。

00:42:21.450 --> 00:42:40.210
<v Janelle  Taylor>You could use that evidence to draw a clear line between us. Me here, on the side of reality, competence, personhood, recognition; her over there, on the side of delusion, incapacity, not quite fully human.
<v Janelle  Taylor>你可以用那些证据在我们之间划出一条清晰的界线。我在这里，站在现实、能力、人格、认知的这一边；她在那里，站在幻想、无能、不够完全人性的那一边。

00:42:40.210 --> 00:42:58.630
<v Janelle  Taylor>But what she was longing for was her childhood home. She missed her mom and dad. She was trying, in her own way, to hold on to them, just as I was trying, against the odds, to hold on to her. Our predicament is exactly the same.
<v Janelle  Taylor>但她所渴望的是她童年的家。她想念她的妈妈和爸爸。她正在以自己的方式试图留住他们，就像我正在试图违背规律留住她一样。我们的处境完全一样。

00:42:58.630 --> 00:43:28.610
<v Ira      Glass>Janelle Taylor-- she is a professor at the University of Toronto, teaching medical anthropology. Her mom, Charlene Taylor, died in 2019. Janelle is collecting this essay and others about dementia into a book. You can find a link to the original academic article that she wrote at our website.
<v Ira      Glass>珍妮尔·泰勒——她是多伦多大学教授医学人类学的教授。她的母亲，夏琳·泰勒，在2019年去世了。珍妮尔正在将这篇文章和其他关于痴呆症的文章整理成书。你可以在我们的网站找到她写的原始学术文章的链接。

00:43:28.610 --> 00:43:38.620
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Can I Help You? OK, here's one last example of a question that has another question lurking behind it. Question goes like this.
<v Ira Glass>第四部分，我能帮你吗？好了，这是最后一个关于背后潜伏着另一个问题的例子。问题是这样的。

00:43:38.620 --> 00:44:09.400
<v Ira Glass>If Matthew scored an average of 15 points per basketball game and played 24 games in one season, how many points did he score in the season? That's a question from the SHSAT, which is a standardized test given to middle school students in New York City. A high score on the SHSAT will get you into one of the eight top public schools in the city-- wonderful schools. A low score will keep you in the regular public school system, where your school may be assigned by lottery.
<v Ira Glass>如果马修每场篮球赛平均得15分，一个赛季打了24场比赛，他在那个赛季总共得了多少分？这是纽约市给初中生参加的标准化考试SHSAT中的一道数学题。在SHSAT中取得高分将让你进入纽约市八所顶级公立学校之一——很棒的学校。低分将让你留在常规公立学校系统，在那儿你的学校可能是通过抽签分配的。

00:44:09.400 --> 00:44:32.510
<v Ira Glass>So the question lurking behind that math question is, Are you good enough? Are you good enough to go to the best schools? And maybe from there, to the best colleges? And from there, to all the advantages you get from that kind of education, including a higher income, maybe a better job, all other sorts of stuff? Kind of a big scary chasm opening up in the Earth behind that innocent little math problem.
<v Ira Glass>所以，那个数学题背后潜伏的问题是：你足够好吗？你足够好到能进入最好的学校吗？进而进入最好的大学？再进而得到那种教育带来的所有优势，包括更高的收入、更好的工作，以及其他各种东西？在那道天真的小数学题背后，简直像是一道巨大的、可怕的深渊在大地上裂开。

00:44:32.510 --> 00:44:33.780
<v Milo Cramer>In 2017--
<v Milo Cramer>2017年——

00:44:33.780 --> 00:44:42.290
<v Ira Glass>For five years, Milo Cramer tutored kids who wanted to leap over that chasm and into those eight elite high schools. It first made Milo feel good.
<v Ira Glass>五年来，米洛·克雷默一直在辅导那些想跨越那道深渊、进入那八所精英高中的孩子们。起初这让米洛感觉很好。

00:44:42.290 --> 00:44:51.630
<v Milo Cramer>Because I thought I was helping children. And I only gradually came to understand that I was really just a fucked-up cog in a larger, fucked-up system.
<v Milo Cramer>因为我觉得我在帮助孩子。但我逐渐意识到，我其实只是一个更大、更混乱系统中一个混乱的齿轮。

00:44:51.630 --> 00:45:17.460
<v Ira Glass>This recording is from a one-person show that Milo did this fall about the kids they tutored. I worry a little that it's going to be hard to get across over the radio what's so special about this show. Most of it is songs-- songs about the kids that Milo tutored-- these very funny and heartbreaking portraits of these middle school and high school kids and Milo's relationship to them. Like, for example, the boy who takes a lot of pleasure denouncing God and the Democrats.
<v Ira Glass>这段录音来自于米洛今年秋天做的一场独角戏，关于他们辅导过的孩子们。我有点担心通过电台传达这场演出的特殊之处会很困难。其中大部分是歌曲——关于米洛辅导的孩子们的歌——这些关于这些初中和高中孩子及其与米洛关系，非常有趣且令人心碎的肖像。比如，那个对诅咒上帝和民主党人感到非常愉悦的男孩。

00:45:17.460 --> 00:45:33.490
<v Milo Cramer>(SINGING) Jason's 16, and he proudly identifies as libertarian. He's a 16-year-old libertarian. I'm kind of afraid of him.
<v Milo Cramer>（歌唱）杰森16岁，他自豪地认同自己是自由意志主义者。他是一个16岁的自由意志主义者。我有点怕他。

00:45:33.490 --> 00:45:45.460
<v Ira  Glass>Milo is not a great singer. They would tell you that themself. Or a skilled musician. But they've written songs in secret since they were the age that these kids are that they're writing about.
<v Ira  Glass>米洛不是个伟大的歌手。他们自己会告诉你这一点。也不是个熟练的音乐家。但他们从这些孩子现在的年纪就开始秘密写歌了。

00:45:45.460 --> 00:46:12.390
<v Ira  Glass>And there's just something in the intentional roughness and sincerity of what they're doing that kind of matches the rawness of these kids and their feelings-- and of Milo's reactions to them. When a girl from Queens named Dana, who's better at math than Milo, and probably should be a scientist or engineer someday, tells Milo that if she does end up in college, she wants to study theater, Milo, who's broke and struggling and wanting to do theater, sings--
<v Ira  Glass>而且在他们所做的故意粗糙和真诚之中，有一种东西与这些孩子们及他们的感受，还有米洛对他们的反应的原始感相呼应。当一个来自皇后区的女孩叫达娜，她在数学上比米洛好，可能以后应该成为科学家或工程师，告诉米洛如果她真的能上大学，她想学戏剧时，破产、挣扎、想做戏剧的米洛唱道——

00:46:12.390 --> 00:46:28.990
<v Milo Cramer>(SINGING) I want to tell her not to! I want to tell her not to! You cannot study theater! You have to study math! You're good at math! You're failing math?! You want to study theater?! Theater doesn't matter! There's a pandemic!
<v Milo Cramer>（歌唱）我想告诉她别去！我想告诉她别去！你不能学戏剧！你必须学数学！你数学很好！你数学不及格？！你想学戏剧？！戏剧不重要！有流行病！

00:46:28.990 --> 00:46:44.460
<v Ira Glass>Lots of the songs in the show are about the kids' anxieties about school and this test and all the pressure they feel from their parents. And they're about Milo trying to figure out not just how to teach them, but what they possibly could say to comfort them. Faith, for example, is a terrible reader.
<v Ira Glass>演出中的许多歌都是关于孩子们对学校和这次考试的焦虑，以及他们从父母那里感受到的所有压力。这些歌是关于米洛在试图搞清楚不仅是如何教他们，还有他可能对他们说什么来安慰他们。比如，费思是个糟糕的读者。

00:46:44.460 --> 00:46:59.650
<v Milo Cramer>(SINGING) Faith says, I think I'm stupid. I can't read. I guess I'm stupid. I get Bs. I must be stupid. I say, I don't think you're stupid. Faith repeats, I'm sure I'm stupid. If you think I'm smart, please prove it.
<v Milo Cramer>（歌唱）费思说：“我觉得我很蠢。我不会读。我想我很蠢。我拿B。我一定很蠢。”我说：“我不觉得你蠢。”费思重复道：“我肯定我很蠢。如果你觉得我聪明，请证明它。”

00:46:59.650 --> 00:47:17.210
<v Milo Cramer>I tell her, intelligence is unmeasurable and different in every individual. Faith just looks at me and says, no. I say, yes. She says, no. She says, no, no, no, no.
<v Milo Cramer>我告诉她，智力是不可衡量的，在每个人身上都是不同的。费思只是看着我说：“不。”我说：“是的。”她说：“不。”她说：“不，不，不，不。”

00:47:17.210 --> 00:47:42.060
<v Milo Cramer>I say, hey, when I was your age, my mom hid all my report cards from me. When I asked her what my grades were, she always told me, you're right where you should be. You're right where you should be.
<v Milo Cramer>我说：“嘿，当我像你这么大时，我妈把所有成绩单都藏起来了。当我问她我的成绩时，她总是告诉我：‘你就在你应该在的地方。你就在你应该在的地方。’”

00:47:42.060 --> 00:47:55.830
<v Ira Glass>Our radio show that is about questions. And to close out the show, I'm just going to play you one more thing. This is one full song from Milo's show about a question that a student faced. It's an essay question.
<v Ira Glass>我们的广播节目是关于问题的。为了结束节目，我再放一首歌。这是来自米洛演出的一整首歌，关于一个学生面临的一个问题。这是一个议论文问题。

00:47:55.830 --> 00:48:16.100
<v Milo Cramer>(SINGING) Divya has to respond to the question, Is Shakespeare's Othello racist? in a five-paragraph essay for her white teacher by Monday. And she says, just tell me the answer, please. I have so much homework this week.
<v Milo Cramer>（歌唱）迪维娅周一必须为她的白人老师完成一篇五段式文章，回应这个问题：“莎士比亚的《奥赛罗》是种族主义的吗？”她说：“只要告诉我答案，拜托。我这周有很多家庭作业。”

00:48:16.100 --> 00:48:30.780
<v Milo Cramer>I need to get this done as fast as possible. Is Othello racist? Yes or no? I'm like, have you read the play? She's like, yes, and I watched the Laurence Fishburne movie.
<v Milo Cramer>我需要尽快完成这个。奥赛罗是种族主义者吗？是还是不是？我说：“你读过剧本了吗？”她说：“是的，我看了劳伦斯·菲什伯恩的电影。”

00:48:30.780 --> 00:48:41.210
<v Milo Cramer>I'm like, great, so what do you think? She's like, I don't know. I'm 15. I'm afraid to say the wrong thing. I'm like, same.
<v Milo Cramer>我说：“太好了，那你怎么看？”她说：“我不知道。我15岁。我怕说错话。”我说：“我也是。”

00:48:41.210 --> 00:48:43.920
<v Milo Cramer>[AUDIENCE LAUGHTER]
<v Milo Cramer>[观众笑声]

00:48:43.920 --> 00:48:56.420
<v Milo Cramer>This stuff is hard to talk about, but you've got to trust yourself, even though you've also got to constantly question and interrogate yourself. Either way, you've got to try. You've got to try. You've got to try, try, try, try, try.
<v Milo Cramer>这些东西很难谈论，但你必须相信自己，尽管你也必须不断质疑和审视自己。无论如何，你必须尝试。你必须尝试。你必须尝试，尝试，尝试，尝试，尝试。

00:48:56.420 --> 00:49:13.010
<v Milo Cramer>I'm desperate to do a good job. Divya's Indian-American mom can hear us in the next room. I do not know what to do. Divya looks at the assignment rubric to see how she'll be graded.
<v Milo Cramer>我非常渴望做好这份工作。迪维娅的印度裔美国母亲能在隔壁房间听到我们。我不知道该怎么办。迪维娅看着作业评分表，看她会被如何打分。

00:49:13.010 --> 00:49:33.410
<v Milo Cramer>She needs a clear, defensible thesis, followed by three unique body paragraphs. I can tell she's overwhelmed. I say, remember, grades don't matter, Divya. Learning can't be measured. Just trust how you feel you did.
<v Milo Cramer>她需要一个清晰、可辩护的论点，跟着三个独特的正文段落。我看得出她被压得透不过气来。我说：“记住，分数不重要，迪维娅。学习是不可衡量的。只要相信你做得很棒的感觉。”

00:49:33.410 --> 00:49:55.520
<v Milo Cramer>She says, maybe grades don't matter if you're rich, but in my family, grades are so important. I think I thought at first that Divya didn't have the words to talk about the play in any nuanced way. But now, I start to think that her understanding is deeper than my own.
<v Milo Cramer>她说：“也许如果你有钱分数不重要，但在我们家，分数非常重要。”我想起初我以为迪维娅没有词汇以任何微妙的方式谈论这出戏。但现在，我开始认为她的理解比我更深。

00:49:55.520 --> 00:50:13.010
<v Milo Cramer>And she might never talk to me about Othello honestly and shouldn't have to. Finally, I decide she just wants me to provide her with some easy answer to satisfy her teacher and get her through the semester unscathed. So I'm like--
<v Milo Cramer>而且她可能永远不会诚实地跟我谈论《奥赛罗》，也不应该被要求这样做。最后，我决定她只是想让我给她提供一些简单的答案来满足她的老师，并让她安然度过本学期。所以我说——

00:50:13.010 --> 00:50:14.690
<v Milo Cramer>(SPEAKING) OK.
<v Milo Cramer>（说话）好的。

00:50:14.690 --> 00:50:16.580
<v Milo Cramer>[AUDIENCE LAUGHTER]
<v Milo Cramer>[观众笑声]

00:50:16.580 --> 00:50:50.260
<v Milo Cramer>Your teacher is either looking for an essay that's like, yes, Othello is very racist. The story of the play is, there's this super professionally and romantically successful Black man all of these white guys are jealous of and cannot handle. That tension is resolved when the white guys trick Othello into murdering his wife, thereby turning him into the brutish stereotype they wanted him to be all along. That the title role was performed in blackface for centuries underscores this.
<v Milo Cramer>你的老师要么是在找一篇像“是的，奥赛罗是非常种族主义的”的文章。这出戏的故事是，有一个非常专业且浪漫上成功的黑人男性，所有白人家伙都嫉妒他且无法应付。那种张力在白人家伙诱骗奥赛罗谋杀他妻子时得到解决，从而把他变成他们一直想要的野蛮刻板印象。标题角色几个世纪以来一直由黑脸表演，这一点强调了这一点。

00:50:50.260 --> 00:51:08.900
<v Milo Cramer>Moreover-- That's a good-- that's kind of a transition word, Divya-- "moreover," i.e. "my next body paragraph will be about." Moreover, Desdemona's whiteness, in contrast, is repeatedly presented as innately good, innocent, and desirable. That's one essay you could write that would get an A.
<v Milo Cramer>而且——那是个很好的过渡词，迪维娅——“而且”，即“我的下一个正文段落将是关于”。而且，德斯德蒙娜的白皙，相比之下，被反复表现为天生善良、无辜和令人向往。那是一篇你可以写出能拿到A的文章。

00:51:08.900 --> 00:51:31.490
<v Milo Cramer>The other essay you could write that would also get an A goes, no, Othello is not super racist. Othello is a flawed attempt at anti-racism, in that it's Shakespeare's only play to center a dynamic Black protagonist. The play was banned in apartheid South Africa for depicting an interracial relationship. Moreover--
<v Milo Cramer>你能写的另一篇也能拿到A的文章是：“不，奥赛罗不是超级种族主义者。奥赛罗是一个反种族主义的缺陷尝试，因为它是莎士比亚唯一以动态黑人主角为中心的戏剧。这出戏在南非种族隔离时期被禁止，因为它描绘了跨种族关系。而且——”

00:51:31.490 --> 00:51:32.230
<v Milo Cramer>[AUDIENCE LAUGHTER]
<v Milo Cramer>[观众笑声]

00:51:32.230 --> 00:51:44.590
<v Milo Cramer>The play's most prejudiced characters are always presented as either stupid, Rodrigo, or evil, Iago. It would be a mistake to conflate the perspectives of these characters with the meanings of the work as a whole. Either of those essays would get As, Divya.
<v Milo Cramer>这出戏最带有偏见的性格总是被表现为愚蠢（罗德里戈）或者邪恶（伊阿古）。将这些角色的观点与作品作为一个整体的含义混为一谈是一个错误。这两篇文章都能拿到A，迪维娅。

00:51:44.590 --> 00:52:13.080
<v Milo Cramer>But what your teacher's reductive yes-or-no prompt does not allow for is an essay that's like, what I think I think, which is something like, Othello is a product and reflection of another culture, Elizabethan England, 400 years old, written at a time when race was just being invented as a system of power. The play later became a cultural export of the British Empire, which colonized Black and Brown people around the world. The play remains a bestseller of the Shakespeare-industrial complex.
<v Milo Cramer>但你老师的还原论式是/否提示所不允许的是一篇这样的文章：我所认为的我所想的，大概是这样的，奥赛罗是另一种文化——伊丽莎白时代的英国，400年前的产品和反映，写于种族刚被发明为一种权力系统时。这出戏后来成为大英帝国的文化出口，殖民了世界各地的黑人和棕色人种。这出戏仍然是莎士比亚工业复合体的畅销书。

00:52:13.080 --> 00:52:36.690
<v Milo Cramer>In other words, Divya, Othello and racism are so indelibly linked, that the question, Is Othello racist? seems to confuse both what racism is and what artworks are. In my opinion, what's really racist, Divya, is that we are required to read Othello for the billionth time, that it's on the curriculum at your Brooklyn public high school, even though the play is boring--
<v Milo Cramer>换句话说，迪维娅，《奥赛罗》和种族主义联系得如此不可磨灭，以至于这个问题“奥赛罗是种族主义的吗？”似乎混淆了什么是种族主义以及什么是艺术品。在我看来，真正种族主义的是，迪维娅，我们被要求第一百万次阅读《奥赛罗》，它在布鲁克林公立高中的课程表上，尽管这出戏很无聊——

00:52:36.690 --> 00:52:42.600
<v Milo Cramer>[AUDIENCE LAUGHTER]
<v Milo Cramer>[观众笑声]

00:52:42.600 --> 00:52:47.670
<v Milo Cramer>--when we could be reading any number of contemporary Black playwrights.
<v Milo Cramer>——当我们本可以阅读任何数量的当代黑人剧作家时。

00:52:47.670 --> 00:52:52.680
<v Milo Cramer>[STRUMMING UKULELE]
<v Milo Cramer>[尤克里里弹奏]

00:52:52.680 --> 00:53:10.580
<v Milo Cramer>(SINGING) Divya responds, don't hate me, but I kind of liked reading Othello. The story is really crazy, and the language is really pretty.
<v Milo Cramer>（歌唱）迪维娅回答：“别讨厌我，但我有点喜欢读《奥赛罗》。故事真的很疯狂，语言真的很美。”

00:53:10.580 --> 00:53:13.720
<v Milo Cramer>[LAUGHTER, APPLAUSE]
<v Milo Cramer>[笑声，掌声]

00:53:13.720 --> 00:53:43.160
<v Ira  Glass>Milo Cramer, in the one-person show, School Pictures, recorded at Playwrights Horizons in New York. To hear more songs for the show or to book them to come to your town, go to milocramer.com. That's Cramer with a C-- milocramer.com.
<v Ira  Glass>米洛·克雷默，在独角戏《学校照片》中，录制于纽约剧作家地平线剧院。要听更多节目中的歌曲或预定他们来你的城市，请访问milocramer.com。那是克雷默的C——milocramer.com。

00:53:43.160 --> 00:54:22.540
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Zoe Chace. The people who put together today's show include Jendayi Bonds, Sean Cole, Michal Comite, Bethel Habte, Chana Joffe-Walt, Katherine Rae Mondo, Stowe Nelson, Safiya Riddle, Lilly Sullivan, Frances Swanson, Christopher Swetala, Marisa Robertson-Textor Matt Tierney, Nancy Updike, Julie Whitaker, and Diane Wu. Our managing editor is Sarah Abdurrahman. Our senior editor is David Kestenbaum. Our executive editor is Emanuele Berry.
<v Ira Glass>好了，我们的节目今天由佐伊·蔡斯制作。今天节目的幕后人员包括詹达伊·邦兹、肖恩·科尔、米哈尔·科米特、贝塞尔·哈布特、查娜·乔夫-沃尔特、凯瑟琳·雷·蒙多、斯托·尼尔森、萨菲亚·里德尔、莉莉·沙利文、弗朗西斯·斯旺森、克里斯托弗·斯威塔拉、玛丽莎·罗伯逊-泰克斯特、马特·蒂尔尼、南希·厄普代克、朱莉·惠特克和黛安·吴。我们的执行编辑是萨拉·阿卜杜拉赫曼。我们的高级编辑是大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆。我们的执行编辑是埃曼纽尔·贝里。

00:54:22.540 --> 00:54:32.220
<v Ira Glass>Original music for the comedian story by Ryan Rumery, who also helped mix the show. Special thanks today to Lauren Kessler. Her book is Finding Life in the Land of Alzheimer's.
<v Ira Glass>喜剧故事的原创音乐由瑞安·鲁梅里制作，他也协助了节目的混音。特别感谢劳伦·凯斯勒。她的书是《在阿尔茨海默氏症的土地上寻找生命》。

00:54:32.220 --> 00:54:40.370
<v Ira Glass>Also thanks to Galya Walt, Michael Rosenthal, Diana Taylor-William, Mike Taylor, Pat Taylor, David Johnson, Rachel Jackson, Tom O'Keefe, and Jolie Myers.
<v Ira Glass>也要感谢加利亚·沃尔特、迈克尔·罗森塔尔、戴安娜·泰勒-威廉、迈克·泰勒、帕特·泰勒、大卫·约翰逊、雷切尔·杰克逊、汤姆·奥基夫和朱莉·迈尔斯。

00:54:40.370 --> 00:54:55.190
<v Ira Glass>Our website-- thisamericanlife.org, where you can stream our archive of over 800 episodes for absolutely free. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks, as always, to our program's confounder, Mr. Torey Malatia.
<v Ira Glass>我们的网站——thisamericanlife.org，在那里你可以免费流媒体播放我们超过800集的档案。《美国生活》由PRX，公共广播交易所交付给公共广播电台。感谢一如既往，我们节目的混淆者，托雷·马拉蒂亚先生。

00:54:55.190 --> 00:55:02.650
<v Ira Glass>You know, he kind of hurt my feelings this morning. We ran into each other. He asked, How am I doing? I started to answer, then he was like--
<v Ira Glass>你知道，他今天早上伤了我的心。我们碰面了。他问我：“最近怎么样？”我开始回答，然后他——

00:55:02.650 --> 00:55:12.240
<v Adam Ray>(SINGING) Well, who cares? Who cares? Who cares? Who cares?
<v Adam Ray>（歌唱）嗯，谁在乎呢？谁在乎呢？谁在乎呢？谁在乎呢？

00:55:12.240 --> 00:55:17.240
<v Ira  Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira  Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带着更多《美国生活》的故事回来。
